{"kind":"article","schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"abstract":[{"text":"Using SwiftUsd from C++","type":"text"}],"seeAlsoSections":[{"anchor":"Using-SwiftUsd-from-C++","generated":true,"title":"Using SwiftUsd from C++","identifiers":["doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/SwiftCxxInteropMemorySafety","doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/SwiftCxxMacros"]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/OpenUSD"]]},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/GettingStartedInCPlusPlus","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Getting Started","anchor":"Getting-Started"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Before you can start using SwiftUsd, you need to add it as a dependency to your Xcode project or Swift Package, and then configure a few build settings.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"aside","style":"note","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Despite the fact that SwiftUsd is a Swift Package, you can use it in a pure-C++ Xcode project\/target, as well as in a pure-C++ Swift Package target."}]}],"name":"Note"},{"type":"aside","style":"important","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Swift Package targets cannot mix Swift and C++ code in a single target. "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"https:\/\/github.com\/swiftlang\/swift-package-manager\/issues\/8945","isActive":true}]}],"name":"Important"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"To do so, follow the steps in "},{"isActive":true,"overridingTitleInlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Getting Started for Swift"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftUsd\/documentation\/SwiftUsd\/GettingStarted#Getting-Started","overridingTitle":"Getting Started for Swift","type":"reference"},{"type":"text","text":". If you’re using a pure-C++ target, you don’t need to change the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"C++ an